WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:10.490
Outcome of the Review of Incentives, Rewards
and Sanctions by NHS England

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:11.490
Paul Baumann
Chief Financial Officer

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.719
Preamble
This item really concludes one of the commitments

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:20.660
that we made in Everyone Counts back at the
start of the year, which was fundamentally

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:27.050
to review essentially the incentives and sanctions
that are in the system, sitting alongside

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.619
the system of pricing that we have, through
carry-off and other measures.

00:00:29.619 --> 00:00:33.089
Overview of the paper
What you have in front of you today is the

00:00:33.089 --> 00:00:36.559
outcome of that particular review for your
consideration.

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:43.460
I think it recognises the vital importance
of a system of incentives and pricing measures,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.270
which do two things, really, one of which
is not to get in the way of transformation,

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:56.930
of change, of improvement in patient services,
but secondly, more actively to actually stimulate

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.619
some of the change that we need in those areas.

00:01:00.619 --> 00:01:01.760
That is what you have in front of you.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.600
It is the product of work that has been undertaken
by the Pricing and Incentives Network, which

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.510
I think is a good example of cross-directorate
matrix working with a very strong clinical

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:15.630
representation in particular, which I think
has helped us enormously to get to where we

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:16.630
have done.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.549
There has been extensive involvement of the
Commissioning Assembly in this, both in the

00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:26.170
form of a dedicated sub-group and the plenary
meeting of the Commissioning Assembly, where

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.329
there was engagement on this particular topic.

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:35.789
Thirdly, it has been subject to wide engagement
in the sector over the July to September period,

00:01:35.789 --> 00:01:41.390
so the things you see in front of you have
widespread support and have been considered

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.859
carefully by people throughout the system.

00:01:44.859 --> 00:01:49.799
They sit alongside the things we have talked
about separately, which are the pricing strategy

00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:53.600
that we are pursuing with Monitor and, in
particular, the engagement around the tariff

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:54.700
for 2014/15.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:00.609
Clearly, tariff needs to sit alongside a process
and system of incentives, to have that combined

00:02:00.609 --> 00:02:01.609
impact.

00:02:01.609 --> 00:02:06.189
I think it is fair to draw out the parallels
between the way we have approached tariff

00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:10.789
for 2014/15 and the way we are approaching
incentives for 2014/15, which is that, essentially,

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:16.560
in 2014/15, we are trying to achieve stability
above all other things, because of all the

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.090
challenges that are in the system.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.739
Both on tariff and on these incentives, we
have not lit the blue touchpaper of vast,

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:26.819
radical changes; we have tried to improve
where there is good evidence that we need

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:29.560
to improve, but there is a degree of stability.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:34.489
Allied to that, I think there is a move to
encouraging local innovation in the way these

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:41.360
things are applied, so that where there is
something that local healthcare partners around

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:47.060
the place wish to implement, they can find
their own way within this framework of underpinning

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.660
them with incentives and, indeed, underpinning
them with tariff prices.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.540
That is the philosophy that is here.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.180
Parity of physical and mental health
I am not going to go through the individual

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.129
proposals because they are set out in the
paper.

00:02:57.129 --> 00:03:01.890
Members of the Board have had opportunity
to input to them at various points in this

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:07.000
process, but obviously, we can discuss any
that are of particular concern or interest.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.730
I would draw to your attention the fact -- and
this links back right to the beginning of

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:14.409
today's meeting -- that I think there is a
noticeable move.

00:03:14.409 --> 00:03:19.400
One of the improvements in this set of incentives
is to recognise the parity between physical

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.040
and mental health.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.169
In just about every dimension of the incentives
you see in front of you, we have brought in

00:03:25.169 --> 00:03:30.390
a stronger component, I think it would be
fair to say, of mental health-related incentives

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:36.250
to try and make sure that that is supported
in an equal way.

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:42.599
Concluding remarks
What I am looking for today is for you to

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:46.540
be content with -- or otherwise -- the conclusions
we have reached from the review, because we

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:51.129
do intend to publish them, as you would expect
of our organisation.

00:03:51.129 --> 00:03:56.550
I would welcome your specific endorsement
of the changes that are set out in the paper

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.969
on the individual measures that we talked
about.

00:03:59.969 --> 00:04:04.110
Thirdly, there is a formality about this,
which is that we welcome your delegation to

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.269
the Executive Team of the finalisation of
this work.

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:11.849
There are, as the paper sets out, a number
of details that still need to be sorted out

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:16.120
within this particular framework, and it is
simply not practical to wait until the next

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:21.190
Board meeting to do all of that, because otherwise,
our planning processes and contracting processes

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:25.720
will not get the right speed behind them.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.190
That is what we are looking for today.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:33.580
I would welcome any support you can give on
those fronts.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:36.920
Q&amp;A
Malcolm Grant: The expectation of the Board

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:42.350
is, first of all, an endorsement of the approach,
by which we mean also commending the process

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:48.280
that has been undertaken up to this point
and the engagement of members of the Board

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:49.280
and others.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.560
Then secondly, about the key messages that
are coming out from the review, and you have

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:01.940
touched upon local flexibility, stability
and the need for it, and also, the move towards

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:05.390
greater parity of esteem between mental and
physical health.

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:11.280
Let me just pause at that level and take comments
before we look at some of the specific items

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.400
that you are asking us to endorse.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.600
Ciarán Devane: Overall, I get the stability
thing.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:25.660
I worry a bit about ambition and how we are
going to get the drive behind some of the

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:30.840
shifts we have, and not just parity of esteem,
but what about supported self-management,

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:36.130
what about general emotional and psychological
support and all of those things?

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:42.620
I also have a bit of a concern that we will
not get that ambition if we delegate some

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:47.690
of this to a sub-committee of the Commissioning
Assembly, because when we started this off,

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:52.560
I remember saying at the time that this was
so big it was something that absolutely had

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:57.160
to be done with the patient voice in the room
as well.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:01.390
I think we have something that can kind of
muddle us through for the year, but I do not

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:06.300
think we have something that is really going
to engage the patient voice with the same

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.971
level of esteem as the commissioning voice
and the clinical voice.

00:06:09.971 --> 00:06:14.000
I think there is something around the process
around taking this forward to the next level

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.440
that we have to do in a very, very different
way.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.300
David Nicholson: I think you are absolutely
right.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:25.110
In a sense, this always was a short-term piece
of work.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:29.930
The bigger piece of work is in relation to
the call to action, where we absolutely want

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:30.930
to look at all of this.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:36.670
Because the issue for me, I think, in all
of this is, what are the behaviours that we

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:39.470
are trying to encourage in this?

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:47.050
We can see in recent newspaper coverage about
the way incentives can sometimes operate in

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:52.530
an organisation to create the wrong type of
behaviours.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.250
I think transparency is a really important
part of this.

00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:02.000
So, one of the things for me is if you take
something like CQUIN, what can happen with

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:08.860
it is, and I do not mean to be rude to finance
directors, but the finance director of a trust

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:15.250
can say, 'I assume I am going to get all my
CQUIN money,' and then build it into their

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:20.750
budget, and then if they do not deliver on
CQUIN it becomes a fining system.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:25.940
So, something that started off for clinicians
as an incentive to do better ends up as a

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:26.940
negative.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:32.170
And it seems to me that you get the wrong
behaviours out of that.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:37.740
Similarly on transparency, especially for
clinicians, I was at a mental health unit

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:43.210
where they had a CQUIN around recovery, and
they were funding a recovery nurse out of

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:45.260
the CQUIN money.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:50.710
And I went into a meeting where that nurse
was working with a group of patients who all

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:55.850
knew that that individual was funded through
CQUIN.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:08:01.240
The patient believed that they could see transparently
the way the money was running through the

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:04.790
system, and they thought it was a fantastic
thing.

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:05.790
It was fascinating.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:09.030
That was about patients having power and control
that you get through transparency, and I think

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:13.530
there is a lot more to do in this to make
it real and get the kind of behaviours in

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:17.530
the right place and hopefully out of the big
work we are going to do for strategy.

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:20.650
We can get into all of this.

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:26.390
Victor Adebowale: I just think that it is
a start.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:34.319
I now understand this better because of what
you have just said, to be honest.

00:08:34.319 --> 00:08:39.560
I think it will get us, in a sense, through
the night, but it does not map onto the earlier

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.339
conversations, and it clearly needs to.

00:08:41.339 --> 00:08:42.339
I do welcome it.

00:08:42.339 --> 00:08:48.610
Again, when I read it, it did not leap out
to me, as much as you have said it, about

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:51.790
the parity of esteem issues, although they
are in there.

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:54.360
Again, we had the conversation yesterday.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:09:02.120
It is great that it is acknowledged in here,
but we need to go a lot further a lot faster.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:09.649
Malcolm Grant: On that basis, I detect reading
body language around the Board -- which is,

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:16.130
after all, the responsibility of the Chairman
-- that we are content, more than content.

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:22.160
I think we are very positive about this, very
positive about the direction of travel, and,

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:28.370
putting it into the context that David has
just spelt out for us, I think happy to endorse

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:33.110
the general approach, but also the specific
recommendations, and to delegate authority

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:38.200
to the Executive Team to finalise the details.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:39.200
We are?

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:40.200
Good.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:40.250
Thank you very much.

